<section xml:id="HW2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Homework 2</title>

  <p><alert> Due Thursday, February 5</alert></p>

<exercises xml:id="hw2-ex">
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        [From the book]: Chapter 11: 4.3bc, 5.1, 5.4ab
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
 <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem A]: Let <m> R</m>  be ring and <m> I</m>  an ideal. Let <m> R[x]</m>, respectively <m> \frac{R}{I}[x]</m>  denote the polynomial ring in one variable over <m> R</m>, respectively<m> \frac{R}{I}</m>. Define: 
<me>
  I[x]:=\{a_0+a_1x+a_1x^2+\dots +a_nx^n\mid a_i\in I, n\in \mathbb N\}
</me>

Prove that <m>I[x]</m>  is an ideal of <m> R[x]</m>  and <m>{\displaystyle \frac{R[x]}{I[x]}\cong \frac{R}{I}[x]}</m>. 
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
 <p> First define a map <m> R[x]\to \frac{R}{I}[x]</m>  using the Substitution Principle (make sure to describe the map). Then compute the kernel and use FIT.  </p> 
</hint>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p> [Problem B]:  If <m> f(x)\in \mathbb C[x]</m>  is a nonzero polynomial of degree <m> n</m>, prove that the ring <m> R={\displaystyle \frac{\mathbb C[x]}{(f)}}</m>  has finitely many distinct ideals.  </p> 
</statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
      <p>
    [Problem C]: It is true that <m> {\displaystyle\frac{\mathbb Z_{100}[x]}{(\overline{2}x-\overline{1})}\cong \mathbb Z_n}</m>  for some integer <m>n</m>. Figure out the value of <m> n</m>  and prove the isomorphism.  
  </p>
  <hint>
    <p>
      Start by writing <m> \mathbb Z_{100}[x]\cong\frac{\mathbb Z}{(100)}[x]\cong\frac{\mathbb Z[x]}{(100)}</m>  using problem A, and then see that your ring is isomorphic to <m> \frac{\mathbb Z[x]}{(2x-1, 100)}</m>  using the second isomorphism theorem.  Then look at the example worked out in class for ideas on how to simplify this even further.
    The answer is NOT <m>n=100</m>. 
    </p>
  </hint>
</statement>
    </exercise>



</exercises>
</section>